            <ArticleTitle>[Dysmenorrhea in the female adolescent. Clinical, biological and therapeutic data].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>253-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The physiology and pathology of dysmenorrhea in adolescent girls has become clear in the past 5 years. Although the prime cause of the disorder has not been identified, the obvious rise in PGF2 alpha prostaglandins is an objective biologic sign. As a result, more specific treatment has become possible with more definite and established care of this &quot;condition&quot; which is often distressing to the teenage girl affected.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
